findings.body:
**Claimed critical; verifier found:** verifier off

Any authenticated caller can pass `accountId` in the JSON body and read another account. The previous behavior always used `caller.accountId` from `auth(req)`; this prefers `body.accountId` with no staff/role check.

Failure mode: `POST/GET` with `{"accountId": "<victim-id>"}` after a normal user session returns the victim account via `Response.json(account)`.

Enforce authorization before using a client-supplied id, for example:

```suggestion
  const body = await req.json().catch(() => ({}));
  const id: string =
    body.accountId && caller.isSupport ? body.accountId : caller.accountId;
  if (body.accountId && id !== body.accountId) {
    return Response.json({ error: "forbidden" }, { status: 403 });
  }
  const account = await db.accounts.findUnique({ where: { id } });
```

(Adapt `caller.isSupport` to whatever role field `auth()` actually provides.)
